    <string name="pdf_need_more">Two or more weigh-ins are needed before a trend can be drawn.</string>
    <string name="pdf_composition_lead">Every figure below is worked from the weight above, the height, the age and the sex recorded on the first page, and each is printed with the range it was judged against and the change since the previous weigh-in.</string>
    <string name="pdf_tape_lead">Circumferences written in by hand with a tape measure, beside the date each was last recorded.</string>
    <string name="pdf_log_lead">Every weigh-in filed on this phone, newest first. The weight column is the figure the scale\'s own display showed.</string>
    <string name="pdf_tape_none">No tape measurements have been written in yet.</string>
    <string name="pdf_foot_1">Weight and impedance are what the device measured. Everything else on this page is calculated.</string>
    <string name="pdf_foot_2">The tissues overlap by design: water, protein, mineral and muscle each count part of the same lean mass.</string>
    <string name="pdf_method_1">What was measured. The weight in this record is the figure the scale\'s own display showed, taken from the Bluetooth broadcast the scale sends while someone is standing on it. The impedance the same broadcast carries is recorded in the weigh-in table and is not used in any calculation here.</string>
    <string name="pdf_method_2">What was estimated. Body composition is computed from weight, height, age and sex using published population equations: Deurenberg et al. 1991 for body fat from BMI, Hall\'s constant of total body water as 73.2 percent of fat-free mass, the reference fractions for protein, mineral and muscle, Yuki et al. 2004 for visceral area, and Mifflin–St Jeor 1990 for resting energy. They describe populations, not this individual.</string>
    <string name="pdf_method_3">How to read them. The equations return overlapping estimates rather than a partition of the body, so water, protein, mineral and muscle together exceed fat-free mass. A single reading also moves with hydration, food, drink, time of day and where the person stood. The trend across weigh-ins is far more reliable than any one of them.</string>
    <string name="pdf_method_4">What this is not. This is a self-recorded log kept on a personal phone, not a clinical assessment. No figure here was measured by a validated method, no diagnosis is claimed, and none should be read as one.</string>
